<<<PLASTIC-DATA:3406638baf86 label="knowledge hop" source="327--graph-of-work-under-the-roadmap/327--graph-of-work-under-the-roadmap.md">>>
### 327
#### Outcome
Ruled plan for graph-ready Plastic: two graphs over one store, graph.md plus nodes/ per intent, needs as the one edge, ledger state with refusals, stateless node agents, in-harness runner; D1 to D48, C1 to C37, sixteen pages; intents 334 to 344 created and roadmap graph-ready-plastic opened.

#### Decisions
Owner rulings from the 2026-09-05 goal and the 2026-09-04 review, recorded as Why-stage
insights on the record:

- D1. Intents stay as the thought work starts from, human or agent, and stay where they are.
- D2. Work executes by planning a graph of delivery under the intent; actions merge into nodes.
- D3. "No work without an intent" stays as the capture rule and loses its ceremonies.
- D4. Plastic is the record of work (knowledge graph) that uses graph engineering to deliver
  work (workflow graph). The two layers are named and bounded (282 carries the boundary).
- D5. The best work is invisible in front: agents execute with savepoints, the human gets a
  short report. This is the base statement every other decision serves.
- D6. Keep the best of prompt, context, harness, loop, and graph engineering, in the owner's
  way.
- D7 (review, point 3). One write per transition: the savepoint line. Everything else renders.
- D8 (review, point 2). `running` plus an expiring lease on the node replaces lock files,
  bridge keys, and the worktree pointer.
- D9 (review, gap 1). Explicit `depends_on` edges are the only scheduling input; `sources` and
  `chain` stay formation and lineage and never drive order.
- D10 (review, point 5). Node kind sets default policy (executor model, which verify node
  attaches, whether a mockup gate precedes); a per-node governance block overrides.
- D11 (review, point 7). Executors load the node packet, built by a deterministic command.
- D12 (review). Executors propose new nodes and edges; they never commit hard dependencies.
- D13 (review). Non-completion is first class: blocked, deferred, needs_decision,
  failed_verification, superseded.
- D14 (review, point 1). Artifacts stay versioned files referenced from the node; a plan
  rejected at review is superseded, never rewritten in place.
- D15 (review, contradiction). Written status with a transition log wins over derived state.

Decisions the research settles:

- D16. Status is the last transition line in the ledger, never a field; file presence becomes a
  doctor consistency check (settles the state contradiction between the two Perplexity reports).
- D17. One edge kind for scheduling, `depends_on` (roadmap syntax `needs`); a node's `depends_on`
  names sibling nodes only; cross-intent edges live only in the roadmap `## Graph`; knowledge edges
  are never cycle-checked and may be reciprocal; execution edges are cycle-checked at every write.
- D18. Four node kinds only: work, verify, decision, research. No kind or edge is added without a
  reader script in the same batch.
- D19 (revised by the second review). One lease file, one ledger, no heartbeat: the intent lease
  (`delivery.lock`) stays for Done and the worktree merge and is touched by the runner at each
  transition; the node lease is the `running` line's `holder=` and `expires=`, written once from
  the kind's default; an expired running node is reclaimed by the runner's sweep with an audited
  line; executors never touch leases; bridge key and pointer retire in G11 after G10 measures.
- D20 (327). Batches are topological layers computed from edges; hand pins are marked overrides;
  critical path is hops. (D20 in 323 named supersedes and lineage; kept as `superseded` state and
  `supersedes` recorded in the ledger line.)
- D21. Verification is a node whose input is the artifact plus criteria; its `done` releases
  successors; `done` is never written on an executor's word.
- D22. `plan.md` and `checklist.md` leave the common path; `spec.md` becomes optional; `outcome.md`
  is generated; `actions/` becomes `nodes/`.
- D23. Files and git stay canonical; no database in this cut.
- D24. The roadmap authors Goal and Graph; tree, batches, and status are rendered.
- D25. The knowledge hop in the node packet ships with 224's 2000-token cap and thirty-delivery
  kill criterion.
- D26 (review B2). Node transition lines skip the ledger dedup; status appends are guarded and fail
  closed; status is the last line in file order.
- D27 (review B7). The trivial path is one work node, no verify node, matrix optional; the ceremony
  bar moves with node count.
- D28 (review risk 1). Only the runner mints node ids; executors propose nodes without ids.
- D29 (review risk 2). A verify node is a separate invocation that receives only the packet; that is
  the cross-harness denominator.
- D30 (review risk 4). A proposed blocking edge into a running node is refused; only the owner and
  the runner write edges.
- D31 (owner, 2026-09-05). Advisors as a real/faux pair are removed. One advisor: the smartest
  model available, or the model set in user config. Plan reviews and verify nodes use it.
- D32 (second review B3). A node with a live sibling runs in its own worktree off the intent
  branch; the runner merges on `done`; siblings with overlapping `files:` never run together; a
  merge conflict is `failed_verification`; `files:` is required for any node with a parallel sibling.
- D33 (second review B6). The ledger wins; INDEX and roadmaps render from it; doctor flags drift.
  Status is per subject (node id or `Intent`); `Done delivered` stays as an alias until G8.
- D34 (second review B7). Direct mode stays the trivial path: a fix in conversation is a day-ledger
  line and a commit; work becomes a one-node intent only when it must survive a session or run
  unattended.
- D35 (second review B8). Programme kill criterion: after Batch 2, a three-node intent whose
  `outcome.md` renders with no hand edit and which resumes from ledger and packet after a forced
  kill; both binary.
- D36 (second review B4). Batch 1 is G1 and G2 only, independent of each other; the stage deriver
  rewrite lives in G3; the doctrine cut lives in G8; edges `G8 needs G9` and `G11 needs G10` exist.
- D37 (second review, scope). Supersedes is a ledger fact plus the `superseded` state with lineage
  computed from the ledger; there is no supersedes edge. A cut against 327's first wording.
- D38 (five-layers comparison, harness item 7). `model=` on every transition line and `gates=` on
  the verify node's transition line, beside `tokens=` and `wall=` (G2).
- D39 (five-layers comparison, prompt item 10). The node packet wraps retrieved text as labeled
  data, never as instruction (G5); the session-start and capture hooks do the same for QMD hits
  and Future-intent hints (G8); artifact comments are harness-side and out of reach.


Rulings from the owner's answers to Q1 to Q10 and from the two graph-checklist reviews, the
four-layers review, and the two design notes (`design--sovereignty-per-harness.md`,
`design--stateless-node-agents.md`).

- D40 (owner, Q1). The knowledge graph and the work graph never affect each other. Node ids
  are local (`n1`, `v1`), minted by the runner, never folgezettel children of the intent id.
- D41 (owner, Q2; amends D22). One authored `graph.md` per intent, shaped like a roadmap file:
  `## Goal` and `## Decisions` (the spec), `## Graph` with nodes and `needs` edges (the plan),
  `## Status` rendered from the ledger, never hand-edited (the checklist). Edges live only in
  `graph.md`; the node envelope keeps `kind`, `files`, `budget`. Node bodies stay in `nodes/`.
  `spec.md`, `plan.md`, and `checklist.md` leave the common path; `outcome.md` is generated.
  Every intent directory has five entries: the record, `graph.md`, `nodes/`, `savepoint.md`,
  `outcome.md`.
- D42 (owner, Q3, Q4, Q6, Q7, Q8, Q9, Q10, all as recommended). Direct mode stays the trivial
  path. Hand batch pins are dropped. The runner stays in the harness session using native
  dispatch; a standalone Ruby runner is parked, not 
